The '''2026 Burawi leadership recall vote''' comprised two parliamentary motions to recall [[Hancil Thea]] from the office of [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]], held on 13 March and 20 March 2026. The first motion, which required approval by seventy-five per cent of Parliament under Article 47 of the [[Constitution of Burawa]], failed to reach the necessary threshold. The second motion, held one week later, achieved the required supermajority after the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] withdrew its opposition in exchange for tax concessions. The passage of the recall vote triggered the announcement of [[snap election]]s to be held within thirty days.

The recall proceedings were accompanied by [[2026 Burawi leadership recall protests|nationwide protests]], which intensified following the second vote. During the announcement of the recall result on 20 March, demonstrators breached the perimeter of the [[Parliament of Burawa|parliamentary precinct]], resulting in a violent confrontation with security forces involving tear gas and water cannons. In the aftermath, Thea announced that snap elections would be called, and declared his intention to stand for re-election.

The recall vote represented the first successful application of the parliamentary recall mechanism against a Leader of the Burawi Republic since the adoption of the 1932 Constitution.

== Background ==

=== The 2025 leadership election ===

{{main|2025 Burawi leadership election}}

The [[2025 Burawi leadership election]] was the first conducted under {{w|ranked-choice voting}}, following the adoption of the [[Leadership Election Reform Amendment]] endorsed by outgoing Leader [[Marik Sahamin]]. [[Hancil Thea]] of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], who had placed second in first-preference voting behind [[Iacob Naq]] of the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]], prevailed through the accumulation of second-preference votes from eliminated candidates.

The result produced a divided government unprecedented in the history of the republic. Thea, leading a party that held only 126 of 1,026 parliamentary seats, assumed executive authority over military and public agencies while facing opposition pluralities in both chambers of Parliament. [[Nacan Badalgen]] of the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], nominated by Sahamin, remained as [[President of Burawa]], creating an executive branch in which the Leader and President represented opposing political formations.

=== Accumulation of controversies ===

The Thea administration's early months were marked by a series of controversial decisions that alienated opposition parties and elements of the general public.

In January 2026, Thea issued a directive requiring military airports to open to domestic civilian air traffic, circumventing the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]] that had restricted domestic commercial flights to major metropolitan airports. The directive restored air service to rural communities that had been required to travel to metropolitan airports to access the high-speed rail network, but was widely interpreted as an assertion of civilian authority over the armed forces and a circumvention of parliamentary authority. The directive drew opposition from the [[Joint Generals Consortium]] and protests from the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] and [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]], who characterised it as militarisation of civilian infrastructure.

In February 2026, Thea commuted the sentence of [[Cabdirashiid Fure]], the former representative from the [[Galbeed River]] area who had been the principal figure in the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal|HSR Scandal]] of 2019. Fure had conspired with other northern rural representatives to secure billions of [[Burawi Hanti|hanti]] in funding for a parallel high-speed rail line between [[Sago]] and [[Lanwaley]], justified by falsified population growth projections. The commutation, which reduced Fure's prison term to time served, was denounced by opposition parties as interference with judicial process and protection of corrupt officials, and as symbolic of Thea's alignment with the interests implicated in the scandal.

These actions, combined with Thea's general unpopularity in metropolitan areas and among public-sector workers, created conditions favourable to a recall effort.

== The recall motion ==

=== Formation of the opposition coalition ===

The recall motion was filed jointly by the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] and the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] on 6 March 2026, following weeks of negotiation between the two parties, which had previously disagreed on the timing and strategy of opposition to the Thea administration. The motion was supported by the [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]], the [[Green Party of Burawa|Green Party]], the [[Party for Equitable Progress]], the [[Northern Socialist Party]], and the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]], forming a coalition of parties representing approximately 754 votes in Parliament.

The [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]], which held 65 seats, declined to join the coalition, maintaining its informal conservative alliance with the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]]. The [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]], with 58 seats, announced it would abstain from the vote pending negotiations with the Thea administration on economic policy.

=== Parliamentary debate ===

Debate on the re call motion occupied Parliament from 8 March through 12 March 2026. Supporters of the motion argued that Thea's actions had demonstrated unfitness for office, citing the military airport directive as unconstitutional overreach and the Fure commutation as corruption. They characterised the recall as a necessary remedy for a Leader who had lost the confidence of the population and the legitimate representatives of the people.

Opponents, led by the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], argued that the recall mechanism was intended for cases of severe malfeasance, not policy disagreement. They warned that removing a Leader through parliamentary supermajority would set a destabilising precedent and undermine the independence of the executive. The [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] echoed these arguments, adding that the will of the electorate, expressed through the ranked-choice system, should not be overturned by parliamentary manoeuvre.

== Voting and results ==

The recall vote was held on 13 March 2026. Under the Constitution, a recall requires a joint vote of seventy-five per cent of Parliament, or 770 votes of the 1,026 total seats.

The motion received 754 votes in favour, representing approximately seventy-three and one-half per cent of Parliament. 191 votes were cast against, comprising the National Party and the Christian Socialist Union. 81 members abstained, including the Industrial Party and scattered representatives from smaller parties.

The result fell 46 votes short of the threshold required for recall. The abstention of the Industrial Party, the Party for Equitable Progress, and the Red Block, combined with the no votes of the Sago Revolutionary Party, proved decisive.

