== Aftermath and ongoing negotiations ==

=== Immediate reactions ===

Thea, addressing supporters following the vote, claimed vindication and called upon opposition parties to cease obstruction and engage in constructive governance. He characterised the failure of the recall as confirmation that his administration retained legitimate authority despite parliamentary opposition.

Leaders of the Socialist and Forward parties expressed disappointment while noting that the seventy-three per cent support for recall demonstrated the breadth of opposition to Thea's policies. They announced their intention to proceed with a second recall vote scheduled for 20 March 2026, citing continued negotiations with the Industrial Party and other potential supporters.

The Industrial Party, through its parliamentary spokesperson, indicated that its abstention reflected dissatisfaction with Thea's economic policies, particularly regarding the classification of essential and non-essential industries. The party indicated openness to supporting the recall or abstaining again depending on concessions offered by the Thea administration.

The Christian Socialist Union reaffirmed its opposition to the recall, stating that the constitutional mechanism should not be employed for partisan purposes.

=== Second vote scheduled ===

Parliamentary rules permit multiple recall votes against the same Leader, provided that each motion is separately filed and debated. The opposition coalition announced on 14 March 2026 that a second recall motion would be submitted for vote on 20 March 2026.

The intervening week was characterised by intensive negotiations between the Thea administration and the Industrial Party, as well as attempts by opposition parties to secure additional support from smaller party delegations.

=== The Industrial Party agreement ===

{{main|Business Tax Reduction Act of 2026}}

On 19 March 2026, the final day before the second recall vote, the Thea administration announced an agreement with the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]]. Under the terms of the agreement, the government committed to reducing the corporate tax rate on private enterprises from 30 per cent to 25 per cent for a period of five years, beginning in the 2027 fiscal year. The concession, valued at approximately 150 billion [[Burawi Hanti|hanti]] in foregone revenue annually, was criticised by opposition parties as a politically motivated bribe to preserve Thea's position.

In exchange, the Industrial Party agreed to oppose the second recall motion. The party's parliamentary leader, [[Liban Qaasim]], stated that the tax reduction represented a meaningful step toward economic liberalisation within the socialist framework and that the party's support for the administration was contingent upon continued reform.

== Second recall vote (20 March 2026) ==

=== Voting ===

The second recall vote was held on 20 March 2026. As anticipated, the Industrial Party's 58 members voted against the motion, significantly reducing the support for recall compared to the first vote.

The motion received 812 votes in favour, representing approximately seventy-nine per cent of Parliament. 214 votes were cast against, comprising the National Party, the Christian Socialist Union, and the Industrial Party. No members abstained.

The result exceeded the seventy-five per cent threshold required for recall, marking the first successful parliamentary recall of a sitting Leader in Burawi history.

